course: dictionary
lesson: 447
title: Arena: a place and a contest
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: An arena is a big place with seats all around, where people come to watch. Read the clues in this sentence: «Gladiators fought in arenas to entertain crowds.» Fighting, crowds and seats tell you an arena is a place for a show.
explain: The word has a second meaning too. In «the political arena», there is no building at all. It means the area where people compete, such as politicians fighting for power. When arena is used with a word like sports or political, look for the meaning of an area of activity.
explain: Test your meaning by putting it in the sentence. «The Colosseum in Rome held about 50,000 people.» If you read arena as a big building with seats, the sentence still makes sense. That is a good sign your meaning is right.
fact: An arena is a big building or open space with seats all around, used for sports, concerts and shows.
fact: «The Colosseum in Rome held about 50,000 people» and had a canvas roof for shade.
fact: In ancient Rome, the arena was the sandy area where «gladiators fought».
fact: Arena can also mean an area of activity where people compete, as in «the political arena».
fact: The phrase «enter the arena» means to join a contest or challenge.
exercise: «Gladiators fought in arenas to entertain crowds.» What is an arena? | A quiet garden | *A big space with seats around it, where people watch | A small room for sleeping | A kind of sandwich
exercise: «The Colosseum in Rome had a canvas roof for shade.» What was the roof for? | Keeping out the sound of the crowd | *Shade | Decoration only | Hiding the lifts
exercise: In «the political arena», what does arena mean? | A real building for sports | A sandy floor for fights | A kind of park | *An area where people compete, such as politics
test: What does «enter the arena» most likely mean? | To go out of a building | To sleep in a room | *To join a contest or challenge | To leave a race
test: Find one word in «Gladiators fought in arenas to entertain crowds» that means to amuse or please. | gladiators | *entertain | crowds | fought
exam: Which sentence uses arena to mean an area of activity? | «The animals were brought up in hidden lifts.» | *«He entered the political arena to campaign for change.» | «The sports arena was full of cheering fans.» | «The Colosseum held about 50,000 people.»
